<h1>Optimizing Software</h1>

<p>Now that you've worked through troubleshooting system issues, it's time to tackle a different challenge: ensuring a computer system runs at its best for the task at hand.</p>

<p>This process is called <b>optimization</b>, making adjustments to improve performance, rather than just fixing errors.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Optimization</b> is the process of making something run better, even if it's already working.</p>
</div>

<h2>Troubleshooting vs. Optimization</h2>

<p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">• <b>Troubleshooting</b> focuses on identifying and fixing problems when something is broken.</p>
<p style="margin: 0;">• <b>Optimization</b> is about fine-tuning a system to run efficiently, even when nothing is wrong.</p>

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; color: #06338D; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
<p><b>Example:</b> If a computer is slow, troubleshooting might involve fixing a software bug, while optimization could involve upgrading memory or adjusting system settings.</p>
</div>

<h2>Meet the Clients</h2>

<p>In Lesson 4, students revisit the same five clients from Lesson 1. Instead of troubleshooting a hardware problem, they'll choose one client and work through optimizing their software.</p>

<div style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; gap:12px; margin:20px 0;">
<div style="flex:1 1 170px; max-width:200px; background-color:#F7F7FB; border-radius:10px; padding:10px;">
